# Git Commit Rules for Cursor

All commits created by the AI must follow Conventional Commits.

## Format

```txt
<type>(<scope>): <summary>
```

Examples:

```txt
feat(auth): add login form validation
fix(download): prevent duplicate file verification
refactor(ui): split launcher layout into smaller components
docs(readme): update local setup instructions
test(verifier): add checksum mismatch coverage
chore(config): update electron build settings
```

## Allowed Types

Use only:

- feat
- fix
- refactor
- perf
- docs
- test
- style
- chore
- build
- ci
- revert

## Scope

The scope must describe the affected module, folder, feature, or domain.

Good scopes:

```txt
auth, ui, api, launcher, downloader, verifier, config, docs, tests
```

Bad scopes:

```txt
stuff, misc, changes, update, fix
```

## Summary

The summary must:

- Be written in English.
- Be concise and specific.
- Use imperative mood.
- Be no longer than 72 characters.
- Not end with a period.
- Describe the actual change, not the intention.
- Not mention AI, ChatGPT, Codex, Cursor, prompts, or conversations.

Forbidden messages:

```txt
update
fix
fixes
fixed bug
changes
final
final fix
wip
temp
misc
cleanup
work
save
checkpoint
```

## Required Workflow Before Commit

Before creating a commit, always run and inspect:

```bash
git status --short
git diff
git diff --staged
```

Then decide whether all changes belong to one logical commit. If changes are unrelated, split them into separate commits.

## Staging

Do not blindly run:

```bash
git add .
```

unless every changed file was inspected and confirmed relevant.

Prefer:

```bash
git add path/to/file
```

or:

```bash
git add -p
```

## Never Commit

- secrets
- API keys
- `.env` files
- debug logs
- temporary files
- local IDE/cache files
- generated junk
- unrelated changes
- broken experiments
